-- title:
--   Ordinarity at λ of a newform's λ-adic representation
-- statement:
--   Let $M \geq 1$ and let $g$ be a cusp form of weight $2$ on $\Gamma_0(M)$ which is a newform in the sense of the project: $g$ is a normalised eigenform (its $q$-coefficient at $1$ is $1$, its coefficients are multiplicative at coprime indices, and they satisfy the recursions $a_{p^{r+2}} = a_p a_{p^{r+1}} - p\,a_{p^r}$ for primes $p \nmid M$ and $a_{p^{r+2}} = a_p a_{p^{r+1}}$ for $p \mid M$), and for no proper divisor $M'$ of $M$ is there a normalised eigenform of weight $2$ on $\Gamma_0(M')$ whose $q$-coefficients at the primes not dividing $M$ agree with those of $g$. Let $\lambda$ be a prime, and $S$ a finite set of naturals containing $\lambda$ and every prime divisor of $M$. Let $O'$ be a complete discrete valuation domain of characteristic zero with finite residue field, complete for its maximal-ideal-adic topology, with $\lambda$ in its maximal ideal. Let $\chi_g$ be a ring homomorphism to $\mathbb{C}$ from [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra M 2 (S \ {λ})`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18), the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb{C}}$ of the space of such cusp forms generated by the operators $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell \nmid M$ outside $S \setminus \{\lambda\}$ and $U_q$ for primes $q \mid M$ outside $S \setminus \{\lambda\}$, with $\chi_g(T_\ell) = a_\ell(g)$ for all primes $\ell \nmid M$ outside $S \setminus \{\lambda\}$; let $\iota$ be a ring homomorphism from the range of $\chi_g$ to $O'$, and write $\iota\chi_g$ for the induced map on the Hecke algebra. Let $\rho$ be an adic Galois representation over $O'$: a free $O'$-module $V$ of rank $2$ with a monoid homomorphism $\rho$ from $\operatorname{Aut}_{\mathbb{Q}}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ to $\operatorname{End}_{O'}(V)$ which is adically continuous, i.e. for each $n$ some finite extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ has the property that every automorphism fixing it pointwise acts trivially on $V/\mathfrak{m}^n V$. Assume that for every prime $\ell \nmid M$ with $\ell \notin S$, every valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $\ell$ a nonunit of $A$, and every $\sigma$ in the decomposition group of $A$ acting on the residue field of $A$ by $x \mapsto x^{\ell}$, the characteristic polynomial of $\rho(\sigma)$ is $X^2 - \iota\chi_g(T_\ell)X + \ell$; assume the residual representation of $\rho$ (the base change of $V$ to the residue field of $O'$) is absolutely irreducible, i.e. irreducible after further base change to an algebraic closure of that residue field; assume $\lambda \nmid M$ and that $\iota\chi_g(T_\lambda)$ is a unit of $O'$. Let $P$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ in which $\lambda$ is a nonunit. Then there are an $O'$-submodule $L \subseteq V$ and an element $\alpha \in O'$ such that $L = O' \cdot b_0$ for some $O'$-basis $(b_0,b_1)$ of $V$; $L$ is stable under $\rho(\sigma)$ for every $\sigma$ in the decomposition subgroup of $P$ over $\mathbb{Q}$; $\rho(\tau)v - v \in L$ for every $v \in V$ and every $\tau$ in the image in $\operatorname{Aut}_{\mathbb{Q}}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ of the inertia subgroup of $P$; $\alpha$ is a unit with $\alpha^2 - \iota\chi_g(T_\lambda)\alpha + \lambda = 0$; and $\rho(\sigma)v - \alpha v \in L$ for every $v \in V$ and every $\sigma$ acting on the residue field of $P$ by $x \mapsto x^{\lambda}$ while lying in the decomposition group.
--
--   This is the ordinarity at $\lambda$ of the $\lambda$-adic representation attached to a weight-two newform with good reduction at $\lambda$ and unit eigenvalue $a_\lambda$, together with the identification of the action of Frobenius on the unramified quotient as the unit root of $X^2 - a_\lambda X + \lambda$; it is the form of the statement used in the modularity-lifting argument (Darmon–Diamond–Taylor, Theorem 3.1(f)), phrased for an arbitrary rank-two adic representation whose Frobenius characteristic polynomials outside $S$ match those of $g$ and whose residual representation is absolutely irreducible. It is used in the study of the local Hecke algebra at $\lambda$, in particular in the results on ordinary points and on unit Hecke eigenvalues.
